=== q05: The inspector's report dated Toronto, 31 December 1885 describes a division of the Blackfoot Agency. Which Indians were detached to form the new agency, and who was appointed to run it?
[PASS] facts 1.00 cited True | 7 tool calls: ToolSearch → search → lookup_entity → get_segment → ToolSearch → search → search | $0.15
The snippet gives the full answer with exact wording.

## Answer

The Sarcee and Stony (Stoney) Indians were detached from the Blackfoot Agency to form a new, separate agency (the Sarcee Agency), and **Major de Balinhard** was appointed acting agent and farming instructor to run it.

**Source:** 1886_4 / p0262-toronto-31st-december-1885 [p. 262] — "Since my last annual inspection a division has been made in the Blackfoot agency, by detaching therefrom the Sarcee and Stony Indians, erecting them into a separate agency, and appointing Major de Balinhard acting agent and farming instructor..."

=== q06: Where was the Coryatsaqua reserve (No. 2), which agency administered it, and what was its area in the 1902 Schedule?
[PASS] facts 1.00 cited True | 3 tool calls: ToolSearch → lookup_entity → open_page | $0.12
**Coryatsaqua Reserve No. 2** was located in British Columbia, in the Cassiar district, on the trail to Hazelton, about two miles north of Lachkaltsap. It was administered by the **Babine Agency**. In the 1902 Schedule of Indian Reserves its area was recorded as **360 acres**.

Source: Schedule of Indian Reserves, 1902, p. 63 (paper_id/page as returned by the reserve registry entry `reserve:coryatsaqua-2`; consistent with the 1900 and 1901 Schedule editions, which also list it at 360 acres).

=== q07: What did the 1929 annual report say about the health of Indians in northern Ontario that winter — which diseases were noted?
[FAIL] facts 0.67 cited False | 9 tool calls: ToolSearch → search → search → get_segment → search → search → search → search → search | $0.16
This confirms the passage found is the main department-wide account (not an Ontario-specific one — the 1929 report doesn't have a separate northern-Ontario health note in this corpus). I'll answer based on that.

## Answer

The 1929 annual report's "Indian Health Supervision" section reports that **during the winter, Indians "in common with the white people, suffered extensively from influenza."** It notes that on the more settled reserves the epidemic was not severe, but "in the northern forests where hunting Indians scatter into widely isolated groups of a few families the disease appeared to attain greater virulence." The department used all available means of transport — including aeroplanes — to get doctors and food supplies to these scattered groups, though it found this "extremely difficult." The number of Indians actually affected was reported as "comparatively small."

